172 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The completed interviews comprised 194 male and 54 female respondents, representing 78.2% and 21.8% of respondents, respectively. The gender distribution reflects the household-level interview methodology, whereby the designated head of household was generally interviewed as the primary respondent. As household heads were predominantly male, this resulted in a higher proportion of male respondents. Therefore, the distribution should be understood as a characteristic of the respondent selection approach rather than as a direct representation of the gender composition of the surveyed households. )
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
173 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (The gender distribut-ion presented in this analysis is based on the sampling frame-work provided to the enumerators rather th- an on the actual gen- der of the respondents interviewed. Consequently, it may differ from the data submitted to the UN-HCR which reflects the gender of the respondents who we- er actually intervie-wed. This methodol- ogical difference may also affect the interpretation of other variables included in the analysis.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
174 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 111 0 R
/E (By location type, completed interviews included 105 out-of-camp cases, 90 in Domiz Camp, 35 in Gawilan Camp, 12 in Domiz Camp 2 and 6 in Akre Settlement. The completed sample included 232 Syrian, 15 Turkish and 1 Palestinian respondent; 231 were recorded as asylum-seekers and 17 as refugees.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
